From: Roman Rikhter <teledurak@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: rtw89: pci: don't take RX buffers from the memory
 reserves

A frame received into a buffer of the data ring goes up in that buffer
when a new one can be allocated for the ring, and is copied otherwise.
The new buffer is allocated with __dev_alloc_pages(), which adds
__GFP_MEMALLOC, so the allocation ignores the watermarks and takes pages
from the reserves that are kept for memory reclaim. The buffers are
16 KiB each, one for every frame that isn't short, and on a router with
little memory, under memory pressure, the reserves run out for those
that need them.

Copying is the fallback already, so add __GFP_NOMEMALLOC: the allocation
keeps to the limits of GFP_ATOMIC, and the frame is copied when it fails.

Signed-off-by: Roman Rikhter <teledurak@gmail.com>
---
diff --git drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtw89/pci.c drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtw89/pci.c
index e519090..b4850cd 100644
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtw89/pci.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtw89/pci.c
@@ -379,7 +379,8 @@ static bool rtw89_pci_rx_swap_skb(struct rtw89_dev *rtwdev,
 		return false;
 	}
 
-	new = rtw89_pci_alloc_rx_page_skb(GFP_ATOMIC);
+	/* the frame can be copied: leave the memory reserves to others */
+	new = rtw89_pci_alloc_rx_page_skb(GFP_ATOMIC | __GFP_NOMEMALLOC);
 	if (!new)
 		return false;
 
